<%= render layout: "guides/frame", locals: { guide: @guide } do %>
  <p>
    Since 2015, a pharmacy&rsquo;s trading partners may only be <strong>authorized trading partners</strong>.
    The rule is simple to state and easy to let slide, especially when a shortage pushes you to a supplier
    you haven&rsquo;t used before. It applies then too.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>What &ldquo;authorized&rdquo; means</h2>
    <p>DSCSA (FD&amp;C Act section 581) defines it for each kind of trading partner:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Manufacturers and repackagers:</strong> a valid FDA registration.</li>
      <li><strong>Wholesale distributors:</strong> a valid license under state law (or from FDA), and
        reporting that licensure to FDA each year.</li>
      <li><strong>Third-party logistics providers:</strong> the same: a valid license and annual reporting to FDA.</li>
      <li><strong>Other dispensers</strong>, such as a pharmacy you buy from or sell to: a valid state license.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to check</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Wholesalers and 3PLs:</strong> FDA publishes the licenses they report in a searchable
        <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cder/wdd3plreporting" rel="noopener">annual reporting
        database</a>, updated every business day. Each line is a license for one facility, so look for the
        facility that actually ships to you.</li>
      <li><strong>State licenses:</strong> the issuing state&rsquo;s online license lookup, usually on the board
        of pharmacy&rsquo;s website.</li>
      <li><strong>Manufacturers and repackagers:</strong> ask for their FDA registration, or check it in FDA&rsquo;s
        drug establishment listings.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>A routine that holds up</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>List every supplier you bought from in the last 12 months, including one-off purchases during
        shortages.</li>
      <li>For each, check the license or registration and save a screenshot or PDF with the date you checked.</li>
      <li>Do the same before the first order from any new supplier.</li>
      <li>Re-check your regular suppliers once a year, and note expiry dates where the lookup shows them.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      If a supplier can&rsquo;t show it is authorized, don&rsquo;t buy from it. If you already have product from
      a supplier you now doubt, treat the product as possibly suspect: quarantine it and investigate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The rest of what applies today</h2>
    <%= render "guides/applies_today" %>
  </section>
<% end %>
